WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.310
Taking a comics character from the printed
page to the big screen isn't as easy as you

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.420
might think, and Guardians of the Galaxy's
Gamora was no exception.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.880
Just ask Charlie Wen, former head of visual
development at Marvel Studios, and take a

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.280
look at some of the designs that didn't make
the cut.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.220
Before 2014, there's a good chance that if
you said the phrase "Guardians of the Galaxy"

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.320
to your average moviegoer, their reaction
would be pretty similar across the board:

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.680
"Who the hell are you guys?"

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.380
And that's exactly what Charlie Wen was banking
on when he started adapting the characters

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.560
from Marvel Comics' long history of spacefaring
weirdos for Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.720
"Nobody knew what Guardians was.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.520
It's very niche.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.660
And so, what was great was it also left it
so that we could do a lot with it.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.400
If you upset some of the hardcore fans, maybe
it's okay because there's not as many of them.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:50.900
Ya know?

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:57.880
What it did do is it allowed for a lot of
exploration to create a Marvel Space Odyssey."

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.560
That freedom to experiment is probably why
the screen version of Gamora, as well as the

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:06.900
concept art Wen created, has very little in
common with the comic book depictions that

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.460
have appeared in the pages of Marvel Comics
over the years.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.220
Some comic book characters are relatively
easy to adapt to the screen, at least when

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.220
it comes to knowing what to keep and what
to junk.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.270
Batman, Spider-Man, and Wonder Woman may have
outlandish comic book outfits, but they also

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.080
feature iconic details that make them recognizable.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:28.560
Captain America without his shield and super-outfit
suddenly becomes a guy who's a little too

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.080
excited every July Fourth.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.740
And Wolverine without his claws is just your
hairy Uncle Lou, back with another case of

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:35.240
Molson.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.040
Gamora, by contrast, doesn't really have any
features that truly define her look in the

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.400
minds of comic book fans, and that presented
a major design challenge for the team.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:45.580
Where do you begin?

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.400
For Wen, it started with archetypes… but
it didn't end there.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.950
"As an archetype, she's this, ya know, lone
assassin that functions on her own.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:59.020
You start there, but then I think you really
need to add in those elements that she needs

00:01:59.020 --> 00:01:59.900
to service."

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.080
While this concept art doesn't seem too out
there, it also doesn't really communicate

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.580
many ideas about who Gamora is or what she
wants or needs.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.180
Gamora's adventuring gear seems like she's
ready for battle, but we can't tell much about

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.560
her character beyond that.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.959
According to Wen, an essential part of the
design process comes not simply from picking

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.640
the right look for the right character.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.140
In fact, it's quite the opposite.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:27.400
"When you're trying to write some kind of
story, paint an image, one of the things that

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.880
you do first is you figure out what your limits
are.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.440
The farthest you're gonna go, where are you
gonna start with, and so it's your contrast.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.180
What is your contrast?"

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.540
That idea of establishing the contrast is
clearly at play in this concept art, where

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:47.139
we see Gamora looking a whole lot different
from any of her depictions, either onscreen

00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:48.599
or on the page.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:52.549
Wen led the team about as far away as they
could from the comics, just to see how far

00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:55.400
they could stretch the idea of Gamora before
it broke down.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.240
With her purple skin, weirdly tapered ears,
and her dimpled, mostly bald skull, these

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.120
versions of Gamora definitely have a passing
resemblance to her adopted dad, Thanos.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.520
But she feels a lot more unsettling and creepy
than you might expect for a big-budget Marvel

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.520
movie hero.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.760
As Wen directed his team to continue exploring
different looks for Gamora's onscreen appearance,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.340
they started moving in interesting new directions.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.400
Case in point: this light-purple version of
Gamora, whose skin features some of the intricate

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.740
markings that would become a part of the final
design's signature look.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:31.220
More importantly, however, is the expression
on her face, which seems to express a lot

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.560
more of what might be going on inside her
head.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.260
Wen told us that Kevin Feige was very interested
in this particular look for Gamora.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.740
But ultimately they decided it was more difficult
to pull off the intricate tech on her face

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:43.740
than it was worth.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.180
As Gamora's look evolved, her character began
to emerge in the concept art Charlie Wen created.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.659
In these images, we see two similar settings
and scenarios for the character, Gamora in

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.980
a crowded sci-fi locale, surrounded by aliens
and other outer space weirdness.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.020
But you can see that even though the Gamora
in this image is undoubtedly cool and on the

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:07.150
hunt, this other one that looks lost in thought
seems to have a lot more in common with the

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.780
version of the character we got in Guardians
of the Galaxy.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:16.780
"You know, she's this super assassin that's
from a different alien race, and connected

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.960
with Thanos, Ronan, Nebula.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.360
So, she's got, again, she's got her own dysfunctional
family to worry about.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.780
She has to have certain vulnerabilities, even
in just her look.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:34.260
You can't just go for this ninja, right, that's
all covered and everything.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.520
There's a part of her that is exposed.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.810
I think the word vulnerable paints it a little
bit better.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:44.390
Also that she needs to be a character that
you can relate to, because she's gonna have

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.140
that kind of connection with Peter Quill,
you know, later on."

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.460
While this image still doesn't look a ton
like the finalized look for Gamora, her purple

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.070
skin is a dead giveaway, it still manages
to express more about the character and the

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:59.060
world of Guardians of the Galaxy than any
of the previous versions.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.680
Seeing Gamora at the bar with her Cheshire
Cat grin seems to make her look dangerous

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.960
and deadly… but also ready to get drunk
thanks to the four-armed barkeep right behind

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:08.520
her.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.500
What's most important is that this version
of Gamora has tons of personality.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:16.580
That's the most important part when designing
meaningful characters that audiences will

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.750
spend a lot of time with.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:23.080
"How do you make her feel like a character
that you would really feel for if something

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:23.880
happens to her?"

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.460
Considering how fans reacted when she died
at the hands of Thanos in Avengers: Infinity

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:33.060
War, it goes without saying that Wen and the
team he led at Marvel Studios completely succeeded

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.900
in answering that particular question.

